  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 305 PM EDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nThe short wave trough and pre-frontal forcing which aided in showers \nalong and south of the US Route 24 corridor earlier today will \ncontinue to shift across the eastern Great Lakes region this \nevening. Isolated showers and thunderstorms have also developed \nduring the mid afternoon across southwest Lower Michigan/far \nnorthern Indiana where some increase in low level frontogenesis \nforcing allowed development along weak confluence zone and also \njust downstream of what appears to be southwest extent of weak \nDPVA.\n\nThe focus for tonight into early Wednesday will be another low \namplitude short wave trough ejecting out of the Dakotas and a \nresultant slow southward sinking baroclinic zone into \nWednesday. Preceding this wave and frontal zone will be another \nsurge of higher low level theta-e air. Isentropic forcing does \nnot appear to be as pronounced as last night, but axis of fairly\nstrong low level fgen forcing and increasing elevated \ninstability should compensate for this with scattered showers \nand isolated/scattered thunderstorms expected to redevelop late \nevening into the overnight hours. Given elevated nature of the \ninstability, severe weather is not expected. Some brief heavy \nrainfall is possible given increasing depth of moisture \nprofiles, long/skinny CAPE profiles, and increasing warm cloud \ndepths. Given quite dry antecedent conditions, significant \nhydro impacts are not expected.\n\nBy mid afternoon Wednesday, scattered shower/storm activity should \nprogressively sink southward as low level fgen axis becomes more \nmuted and shifted south. This will occur as some recovery in mid \nlevel heights occurs upstream in response to stronger upper \ntrough/cut-off low feature lifting northeast out of the Four Corners \nregion. \n\nAnother round of active weather is expected Thursday and Friday as \nthe cut-off upper low begins to phase with a more dynamic northern \nstream upper level trough digging southeast out of south central \nCanada. This phasing system will likely result in cyclogenesis \noverhead late Thursday with some favorable upper jet dynamics \nalso coming into play. This strongly forced setup should support\nanother round of showers and thunderstorms Thursday afternoon \nand Thursday night before a strong accompanying cold front \nsweeps across the area. With the tendency to signals of better \nphasing, sfc reflection is likely to take a track closer to \nguidance that has been advertising a northerly track across \nIN/NW OH/SE Lower MI. This could provide a brief temporal window\nof weak near surface based CAPE across at least southeast \nportions of the forecast area late Thursday afternoon and more \nlikely Thursday evening. Uncertainty remains with just how far \nnorth warm frontal feature can progress Thursday night but given\nrobust kinematic profiles may need to monitor for isolated \nstrong/severe threat Thursday evening, especially south of US \n24. Given progression of features and dry antecedent conditions,\nwidespread hydro concerns are not expected. PWATs will be \nhighly anomalous on Thursday however, so some localized heavier \nrainfall amounts are possible.\n\nFriday still appears dismal with blustery conditions developing on \ncold side of this deepening system. Peak afternoon gusts into the 35 \nto 40 mph range still appear possible given deep post-frontal \nmixing. Some consideration may be given to a wind advisory \ndownwind of Lake Michigan, but uncertainty remains in this \nregard. Some lake effect rain showers will also be likely \nalthough duration of deeper lake induced instability profiles \nappears somewhat limited to Friday morning/early PM. Friday and \nSaturday will feature temps well below normal with nighttime \nlows in the 30s. Along with the better phased system comes the \npotential of a slightly quicker eastward progression of sfc \nanticyclone late Friday night with an attendant frost potential\nFriday night and Saturday night. Flow deamplification quickly \nensues to start next work week which should result in \ntemperatures moderating back to slightly above normal by Monday-\nTuesday.",
